TASK:{"title":"Engineering Ethics Report: To Ship or Not to Ship","overview":"Individual engineering ethics report on the 'To Ship or Not to Ship' scenario. Students analyse Rachel's decision to ship potentially defective servers to a government health department, discuss stakeholder decision-making, argue for and against options using reputable sources and external case studies, and recommend a justified course of action. Report requires abstract, introduction, body, conclusion and consistent referencing; maximum 1,500 words excluding abstract, references and figures/tables."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction — Identifies ethical issues and broad links to engineering design. Discusses context of ethical issues in engineering organisations. Identifies the relevant stakeholders when considering the ethical issues.","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":10,"max":10,"desc":"Several ethical issues and their significance identified, described, in the context of engineering design, understanding of BOTH context in engineering organisations and stakeholder interest is demonstrated."},{"label":"Very good","min":8,"max":8,"desc":"Several ethical issues identified, described, and linked to engineering design, understanding of context in engineering organisations OR stakeholder interest is demonstrated."},{"label":"Good","min":6,"max":6,"desc":"Few ethical issues identified, described, and linked to engineering design, but understanding of context and stakeholders is limited."},{"label":"Satisfactory","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Few ethical issues identified and described, but the context and stakeholders are not identified."},{"label":"None","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"No ethical issues identified."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Balanced Discussion — Discusses objective arguments for and against decisions of the ethical issues. Draws a conclusion on how stakeholders should make decisions about the issues.","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":10,"max":10,"desc":"Objective arguments are made with significant depth, leading to conclusions on decision making recommendations for several different stakeholders."},{"label":"Very good","min":8,"max":8,"desc":"Objective arguments are made with appropriate depth. Conclusions are made on decision making, though lack of consideration of different stakeholders."},{"label":"Good","min":6,"max":6,"desc":"Arguments are made, though they lack depth or are based on personal opinion. Conclusions are made on decision making, though lack of consideration of different stakeholders."},{"label":"Satisfactory","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Ideas are presented, though they lack the depth required to build arguments. Conclusion is made with no apparent link to the arguments."},{"label":"None","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"No arguments presented. No conclusions drawn."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Compelling Argument that supports conclusion — The report forms a compelling argument, which justifies the conclusion. The body of the report follows a logical structure, with paragraphs linked and content ordered in a way that is easy to follow and support the building of the argument. Arguments demonstrate a systematic understanding of the ethical issues for all stakeholders.","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":10,"max":10,"desc":"Arguments are compelling, supported by the report structure. The conclusion is well supported by the arguments. The arguments demonstrate a systematic understanding of the ethical issues for all stakeholders."},{"label":"Very good","min":8,"max":8,"desc":"Arguments are compelling, supported by the report structure. The arguments support the conclusion, demonstrating a sound understanding of ethical issues."},{"label":"Good","min":6,"max":6,"desc":"Arguments are logically structured, though are not compelling. Conclusion is not well supported by the arguments presented."},{"label":"Satisfactory","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Arguments are difficult to follow. Link to conclusion is unclear."},{"label":"None","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Arguments are not evident. No conclusions drawn."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Research and Evidence — Arguments are substantiated with adequate references and qualitative/quantitative evidence. Research demonstrates a balanced, in-depth understanding of the topic.","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":10,"max":10,"desc":"Arguments are well substantiated with references as well as both qualitative and quantitative evidence. Evidence of research demonstrates a balanced and in-depth understanding of the topic and its ethical issues."},{"label":"Very good","min":8,"max":8,"desc":"Arguments are substantiated with adequate references and some qualitative/quantitative evidence. Research demonstrates a good understanding of the topic."},{"label":"Good","min":6,"max":6,"desc":"Arguments are substantiated with some relevant references and qualitative OR quantitative evidence. Research demonstrates a moderate understanding of the topic."},{"label":"Satisfactory","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Limited references that lack relevance to arguments, showing limited evidence of research."},{"label":"None","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"No references, or other evidence of research to support arguments."}]},{"id":"C5","name":"Report Structure, Format and Language — The report includes appropriate sections which serve their respective functions well: abstract, introduction, body, and conclusion. Maps, figures, tables, and any visual elements are correctly used to support written discussions. The writing is free from any grammatical errors. The expression is fluent, has appropriate word choice, is concise, easy to read and understand. References are well-structured and appropriate providing strong support of the motivations in the report.","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":10,"max":10,"desc":"The report is fluently written, has no incorrect uses of language, uses several types of visual elements competently, and uses relevant references extensively."},{"label":"Very good","min":8,"max":8,"desc":"The report is fluently written, has no incorrect uses of language, uses several types of visual elements competently, and uses relevant references."},{"label":"Good","min":6,"max":6,"desc":"The report is fluently written, has no incorrect uses of language, uses limited visual elements, and a few relevant references."},{"label":"Satisfactory","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"The report is fluently written, has few incorrect uses of language, very limited visual elements, and few, mostly irrelevant references."},{"label":"None","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"The report has incorrect uses of language, has no visual elements, expression is not fluent, and references are missing."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"INTRODUCTION"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"INTRODUCTION"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"DICUSSION: To Ship or Not to Ship"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"DICUSSION: To Ship or Not to Ship"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"RECOMMENDATION"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"The Most Ethical Path"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"The Most Ethical Path"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"The Most Ethical Path"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"RECOMMENDATION"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"The Most Ethical Path"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"References"},{"id":"R12","met":"yes","where":"The Most Ethical Path"},{"id":"R13","met":"yes","where":"ABSTRACT"},{"id":"R14","met":"yes","where":"INTRODUCTION"},{"id":"R15","met":"yes","where":"CONCLUSION"},{"id":"R16","met":"yes","where":"DICUSSION: To Ship or Not to Ship"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"DICUSSION: To Ship or Not to Ship","issue":"Typographical error in heading ('DICUSSION' instead of 'DISCUSSION')."},{"where":"DICUSSION: To Ship or Not to Ship","issue":"Grammatical errors: missing noun in 'The economic benefits of could very well be' and garbled syntax in 'have her company might go under'."},{"where":"RECOMMENDATION","issue":"Rachel as a QA engineer does not possess executive authority to reject client contracts; her operational remit is to refuse quality sign-off and recommend withholding shipment."}],"overall":"This is a well-argued ethics report that effectively dismantles Rachel's rationalisation regarding data loss by drawing upon the NHS IT failure and EA Code of Ethics. The balance of commercial and ethical arguments is compelling. Incorporating visual risk models, expanding the reference list, and correcting proofreading slips would elevate the submission further.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Excellent","mark":10.0,"scale":10.0,"points":20.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The introduction clearly identifies the ethical dilemma of product quality and safety versus commercial pressure in engineering design. It demonstrates a strong understanding of engineering organisational dynamics (fast release cycles, competitive market) and explicitly identifies key stakeholders, including the QA engineer, the company, chip suppliers, the health department, and end-users relying on healthcare infrastructure.","evidence":[{"where":"INTRODUCTION","quote":"The relevant stakeholders in this scenario include Rachel, Rachel’s company, the chip contract company, the government health department, and the many people who will use"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Excellent","mark":10.0,"scale":10.0,"points":20.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Objective arguments are developed in significant depth, examining market survival, competitor behaviour, and clinical safety. The discussion successfully leads to clear, actionable conclusions and recommendations addressing different stakeholders, including Rachel (raising QA concerns, refusing sign-off) and company leadership (extending development cycles and transparent client communication).","evidence":[{"where":"RECOMMENDATION","quote":"Rachel’s company should delay their launches or switch to a longer product cycle until they have the resources to achieve a six-month product cycle."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Excellent","mark":10.0,"scale":10.0,"points":20.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The argument is exceptionally compelling and logically structured. The student effectively refutes Rachel's rationale regarding data loss versus physical harm by demonstrating how healthcare IT failures directly cause patient mortality. The progression from market pressures to health consequences, professional ethical obligations under the EA Code, and business reputation forms a cohesive, systematic justification for not shipping.","evidence":[{"where":"The Most Ethical Path","quote":"While data loss is always bad, the impacts it can have in the healthcare sector are unparalleled."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Very good","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The submission substantiates arguments with relevant qualitative and quantitative evidence, notably integrating the NHS HSSIB IT failure investigation and the Volkswagen emissions scandal alongside the Engineers Australia Code of Ethics. While the external cases are effectively analysed and pertinent, the overall reference list is modest (four sources), reflecting a very good rather than extensive research base.","evidence":[{"where":"The Most Ethical Path","quote":"In late 2023, the Health Services Safety Investigations Body (HSSIB) investigated a series ofIT system failures in NHS hospitals across England."}]},{"id":"C5","level":"Good","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":12.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report follows a clear structure (abstract, introduction, discussion, conclusion, recommendations, references) and uses IEEE citation formatting. However, no visual elements (such as decision trees, risk matrices, or tables) are included. Additionally, there are minor proofreading errors, including a misspelled section heading ('DICUSSION') and syntax slips ('benefits of could', 'have her company might go under').","evidence":[{"where":"DICUSSION: To Ship or Not to Ship","quote":"DICUSSION: To Ship or Not to Ship"}]}]}
